      <header class="sec-head">
        <p class="eyebrow">The predecessor</p>
        <h2>PatchCleaner was the answer for a decade</h2>
        <p class="sec-intro">
          If you have searched for this before you have probably already found <a
            href="https://www.homedev.com.au/free/patchcleaner"
            target="_blank"
            rel="noopener">PatchCleaner</a> by John Crawford. Free, simple, the go-to tool for
          this for years. Most of what InstallerClean does, PatchCleaner did first.
        </p>
      </header>

      <div class="prose">
        <h3>It excludes Adobe by default</h3>
        <p>
          PatchCleaner was built around a list of installers it was confident it could safely
          identify. Adobe's <span class="mono">.msp</span> patches did not match cleanly with
          what the tool was looking for, so they were excluded by default. That was the right
          call in 2016. Today, on machines with Acrobat installed, the Installer cache is mostly
          Adobe patches, and this is the comment that keeps coming up:
        </p>
        <blockquote class="pull">
          <p>
            I've downloaded Patchcleaner to delete the orphaned .msp files... 29 GB of the files
            are "excluded by filters", so Patchcleaner doesn't seem to help.
          </p>
          <span class="pull-cite">HeatherBunny1111, r/techsupport</span>
        </blockquote>

        <h3>It has not been updated since March 2016</h3>
        <p>
          The last release was 3 March 2016. The source is closed, so no one else has been able
          to pick it up. A decade of Windows changes have happened around the 2016 binary.
        </p>
      </div>

      <p class="close">
        InstallerClean is the modern open-source take on the same idea. It catches the Adobe
        patches PatchCleaner skips, it ships on .NET 10, the source is on GitHub, and VirusTotal
        comes back clean across every engine.
      </p>
